#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059385 :question/id #uuid "9096883e-6dd0-5dd1-8e9c-176775edadba" :question/math-academy-id "e-10785" :question/problem "Which inequality corresponds to the interval $x \\in (1, 5)?$" :question/worked-solution "Every interval can be expressed as an inequality. We use the following notations:\n\n- Parentheses ${\\color{red}{(}}\\phantom{0} \\phantom{0}{\\color{red}{)}}$ to indicate that the endpoints are *not* included.\n\n- Brackets ${\\color{blue}{[}}\\phantom{0} \\phantom{0}{\\color{blue}{]}}$ to indicate that the endpoints *are* included.\n\nOur interval contains only parentheses. So, the endpoints are *not* included. \n\n$$\nx\\in {\\color{red}{(}}1, 5{\\color{red}{)}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our interval can be expressed as the following inequality:\n\n$$\n1 \\,\\,{\\color{red}{<      }}\\,\\,      x  \\,\\,{\\color{red}{<      }}\\,\\,      5\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059386 :question/id #uuid "b828f144-b8b8-5eda-94f1-43cccdcb1aaa" :question/math-academy-id "e-10786" :question/problem "Write the interval $y\\in\\big(-4, \\sqrt{2}\\big]$ as an inequality." :question/worked-solution "Every interval can be expressed as an inequality. We use the following notations:\n\n- Parentheses ${\\color{red}{(}}\\phantom{0} \\phantom{0}{\\color{red}{)}}$ to indicate that the endpoints are *not* included.\n\n- Brackets ${\\color{blue}{[}}\\phantom{0}\\phantom{0}{\\color{blue}{]}}$ to indicate that the endpoints *are* included.\n\nFor the given interval \n\n$$\n{\\color{red}{{\\big(}}} -4, \\sqrt2\\, {\\color{blue}{\\big]}}\n$$\n\nwe note the following:\n\n- The left endpoint $-4$ is *not* included in the interval. Therefore, we use ${\\color{red}{\\lt}}\\,.$\n\n- The right endpoint $\\sqrt2$ *is* included in the interval. Therefore, we use ${\\color{blue}{\\leq      }}\\,.$\n\nTherefore, the inequality that corresponds to the given interval is \n\n$$\n-4 \\,\\,{\\color{red}{\\lt  }}\\,\\,   y \\,\\,{\\color{blue}{\\leq}}\\,\\, \\sqrt 2.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059387 :question/id #uuid "45d05c7d-e278-552f-a524-926d4a57d870" :question/math-academy-id "e-10787" :question/problem "What interval corresponds to the segment shown below?\n\n![](images/43/430481f9d0863b0bb5602f949e6f994d8007ea29fdd741a485d880df8d5d2dec.png)" :question/worked-solution "Every inequality can be expressed as an interval. We use the following notations:\n\n- Parentheses ${\\color{red}{(}}\\phantom{0} \\phantom{0}{\\color{red}{)}}$ if the endpoints are *not* included.\n\n- Brackets ${\\color{blue}{[}}\\phantom{0} \\phantom{0}{\\color{blue}{]}}$ if the endpoints *are* included.\n\nThe given interval can be represented by the following inequality:\n\n$$\n-2\\,\\, {\\color{blue}{\\leq}}  \\,\\, x \\,\\, {\\color{red}{\\lt}} \\,\\, 2\n$$\n\nWe can express our inequality in interval notation as follows:\n\n$$\nx\\in {\\color{blue}{[}}-2,2{\\color{red}{)}}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059388 :question/id #uuid "6d4bd0a2-0961-54c1-b781-b470d420d848" :question/math-academy-id "e-10816" :question/problem "Express the inequality $1 \\le x \\le4$ in interval notation." :question/worked-solution "Every inequality can be expressed as an interval. We use the following notations:\n\n- Parentheses ${\\color{red}{(}}\\phantom{0} \\phantom{0}{\\color{red}{)}}$ if the endpoints are *not* included.\n\n- Brackets ${\\color{blue}{[}}\\phantom{0} \\phantom{0}{\\color{blue}{]}}$ if the endpoints *are* included.\n\nOur inequality contains only \"or equal to\" symbols. So, the endpoints $1$ and $4$ *are* included. \n\n$$\n1\\,\\, {\\color{blue}{\\le}} \\,\\, x \\,\\, {\\color{blue}{\\le}} \\,\\, 4\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express our inequality in interval notation as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}{[}}1,4{\\color{blue}{]}}\n$$\n\nMore formally, we can also write\n\n$$\nx\\in [1,4].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059958 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1b3d8a45-fe13-581e-9e3f-055e9482e906" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[-10,3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482865} {:answer/id #uuid "2eee8041-f4fa-545a-a3f7-4d63c35b6ba8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-10,3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482892} {:answer/id #uuid "3dacba62-f7cd-5fb4-b7ac-b6a55cdc2a73" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-10,3]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482917} {:answer/id #uuid "982b05bc-9e84-5fbb-95ab-465cc3b39a18" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\infty ,3]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483074} {:answer/id #uuid "b9d2aab1-6872-5e77-8697-9067584fdf64" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[-10,3]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483125}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b9d2aab1-6872-5e77-8697-9067584fdf64" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[-10,3]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483125} :answer-field/id #uuid "bbff7b41-8a1b-509f-91ec-dd43bc73c6e6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186056107}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "5bdfaded-c508-56ef-8657-15a990f7ff66" :question/math-academy-id "q-102200" :question/problem "The inequality $-10\\le x\\le 3$ can be expressed in interval notation as" :question/worked-solution "Every inequality can be expressed as an interval. We use the following notations:\n\n- Parentheses $()$ if the endpoints are *not* included.\n\n- Brackets $[]$ if the endpoints *are* included.\n\nOur inequality contains only \"or equal to\" symbols. So, the endpoints $-10$ and $3$ *are* included. \n\n$$\n-10\\,\\,\\le \\,\\,x\\,\\,\\le \\,\\,3\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express our inequality in interval notation as follows:\n\n$$\n[-10,3]\n$$\n\nMo